// MUST FAIL: two arms of a `.match` with genuinely different types.
// src/sema/sema_join.zen's own header states the rule: "two arms of
// genuinely different types mean something the language cannot express —
// inventing a join for it would hide the mistake rather than report it."
//
// THE SELF-HOSTED COMPILER DOES NOT REPORT IT. `join` correctly declines
// to invent a supertype and returns the first arm's type; nothing then
// says the second arm disagreed. `merged_or_first`'s fall-through `Ok(a)`
// is where the diagnostic belongs, and cannot raise one today because
// `join` is not passed a span.
//
// WHY THIS IS WORSE THAN A MISSING MESSAGE. With a `str` second arm the
// emitted C returns a `zg_str` from an `int32_t` function and cc rejects
// it, so the mistake surfaces as a C error full of mangled names. With a
// `bool` second arm — this file — the C compiles, the program RUNS, and
// prints 0. A wrong answer that looks plausible, which is why no oracle
// caught it: a corpus test would never contain mismatched arms, and no
// must-fail test covered them until 2026-08-10.
//
// The bootstrapper reports `expected i32, found bool` at 24:18.
f = (b: bool) i32 {
    b.match({
        true  => 1,
        false => false,
    })
}

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, ArgError> {
    println("{}", f(false));
    Ok(0);
}
